1
00:00:01,732 --> 00:00:02,727
3 products.

2
00:00:02,886 --> 00:00:04,313
6 upload sheets each.

3
00:00:04,461 --> 00:00:05,288
1 command.

4
00:00:05,434 --> 00:00:07,223
And an assistant your team can ask.

5
00:00:07,369 --> 00:00:09,542
This is supplier catalogue automation,

6
00:00:09,569 --> 00:00:11,141
built for FCI London.

7
00:00:11,795 --> 00:00:12,550
By hand,

8
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,532
the Richard bed means finding it in

9
00:00:14,295 --> 00:00:16,961
a 373-page price list,

10
00:00:16,999 --> 00:00:20,134
then typing 2,340 price rows.

11
00:00:20,813 --> 00:00:21,339
Here,

12
00:00:21,306 --> 00:00:23,238
the suppliers' own files go in:

13
00:00:23,277 --> 00:00:25,270
Cattelan's PDF price list,

14
00:00:25,272 --> 00:00:27,830
and Franco Ferri's spreadsheet and swatch card.

15
00:00:27,976 --> 00:00:29,933
A short job file names the product,

16
00:00:29,972 --> 00:00:31,844
and 1 command does the rest.

17
00:00:32,495 --> 00:00:34,861
Richard's page holds 70 prices:

18
00:00:34,899 --> 00:00:35,991
5 sizes,

19
00:00:35,945 --> 00:00:37,217
10 cover columns,

20
00:00:37,195 --> 00:00:38,419
and a storage version.

21
00:00:38,565 --> 00:00:42,385
Cattelan's online catalogue supplies the 356 colours

22
00:00:42,147 --> 00:00:43,575
behind those columns.

23
00:00:43,721 --> 00:00:47,397
So 70 prices become 2,340 rows,

24
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:48,599
copied exactly.

25
00:00:49,254 --> 00:00:50,369
Burt is modular,

26
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,172
so each layout is the sum of

27
00:00:51,934 --> 00:00:52,917
its modules.

28
00:00:53,076 --> 00:00:54,179
698,

29
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:55,753
plus 320,

30
00:00:55,804 --> 00:00:57,725
plus 564,

31
00:00:57,751 --> 00:00:59,696
plus 731:

32
00:00:59,831 --> 00:01:02,317
£2,313.

33
00:01:03,012 --> 00:01:05,522
Photos stay at the supplier's full resolution,

34
00:01:05,512 --> 00:01:08,094
cropped square and saved with the supplier's

35
00:01:07,856 --> 00:01:09,308
own JPEG settings.

36
00:01:09,455 --> 00:01:11,135
Measured against the originals,

37
00:01:11,101 --> 00:01:14,596
every photo scores between 56 and 65

38
00:01:14,358 --> 00:01:15,245
decibels:

39
00:01:15,464 --> 00:01:16,628
visually identical,

40
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:17,649
even zoomed in.

41
00:01:18,295 --> 00:01:20,155
Cattelan's swatches are downloaded.

42
00:01:20,362 --> 00:01:22,162
Franco Ferri publishes none,

43
00:01:22,225 --> 00:01:25,420
so the tool reuses the 162 FCI

44
00:01:25,374 --> 00:01:26,525
already hosts,

45
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,616
and lists the 34 still needed.

46
00:01:29,281 --> 00:01:31,814
NVIDIA's Nemotron writes the descriptions from the

47
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:32,872
supplier's facts,

48
00:01:32,851 --> 00:01:34,975
and each one is checked before it's

49
00:01:34,738 --> 00:01:35,397
used.

50
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:36,786
To test it,

51
00:01:36,825 --> 00:01:37,952
I added Thomas,

52
00:01:37,894 --> 00:01:39,743
a bed FCI built by hand.

53
00:01:39,889 --> 00:01:41,305
Every size matches,

54
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,238
the base price matches FCI's live page,

55
00:01:44,288 --> 00:01:46,930
and it found 41 colours FCI still

56
00:01:46,692 --> 00:01:49,034
lists that Cattelan no longer offers.

57
00:01:49,706 --> 00:01:52,311
The team works in Google Drive Price lists

58
00:01:52,074 --> 00:01:53,153
go in a folder,

59
00:01:53,155 --> 00:01:55,064
and products are rows in a sheet.

60
00:01:55,199 --> 00:01:57,011
A new catalogue is picked up by

61
00:01:56,773 --> 00:01:57,552
its name,

62
00:01:57,566 --> 00:01:58,922
with no setup change,

63
00:01:58,888 --> 00:02:00,809
and every result is saved back to

64
00:02:00,571 --> 00:02:01,242
Drive,

65
00:02:01,196 --> 00:02:01,963
by date.

66
00:02:02,624 --> 00:02:04,857
Each run compares every price with the

67
00:02:04,619 --> 00:02:05,434
last one,

68
00:02:05,545 --> 00:02:07,790
and writes only the rows that changed,

69
00:02:07,828 --> 00:02:08,860
ready to upload.

70
00:02:09,006 --> 00:02:10,146
Re-running Thomas:

71
00:02:10,148 --> 00:02:12,129
560 unchanged.

72
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:14,103
And anyone can just ask.

73
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:16,602
The assistant answers from the run results,

74
00:02:16,593 --> 00:02:17,696
never from memory.

75
00:02:17,843 --> 00:02:18,514
Richard,

76
00:02:18,492 --> 00:02:19,427
size D,

77
00:02:19,441 --> 00:02:20,593
in glove leather:

78
00:02:20,751 --> 00:02:21,771
3 prices,

79
00:02:21,821 --> 00:02:23,321
each checked against the data.

80
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:26,025
Asking it to re-run a product only

81
00:02:25,788 --> 00:02:27,012
proposes the run.

82
00:02:27,158 --> 00:02:29,656
Nothing happens until someone presses Confirm.

83
00:02:29,814 --> 00:02:31,483
Then it re-prices from Drive,

84
00:02:31,581 --> 00:02:33,202
and saves the results back.

85
00:02:33,855 --> 00:02:35,968
Anything a person should check goes into

86
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:36,545
a report.

87
00:02:36,704 --> 00:02:38,913
One command folds the rows back into

88
00:02:38,675 --> 00:02:41,029
the 70 prices on the PDF page

89
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:42,026
for a quick hand check,

90
00:02:42,064 --> 00:02:45,343
and 192 tests run on every push.

91
00:02:45,994 --> 00:02:48,179
85 to 90 percent of the work,

92
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:48,924
automated.

93
00:02:49,059 --> 00:02:50,775
A new product is a row in

94
00:02:50,537 --> 00:02:51,268
a sheet,

95
00:02:51,330 --> 00:02:53,058
and a new supplier is a new

96
00:02:52,821 --> 00:02:53,456
reader.

